  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale:\nThis plan employs a synergistic optimization framework combining gradient-based methods with mathematical programming to minimize the AC1 upper bound. The core idea is to:\n\n1. Maintain a dense representation (\u03bc) and sparse component (\u03bd) for reduced dimensionality\n2. Use projected gradient steps with adaptive directional strategy\n3. Incorporate random structured initialization to escape local minima\n4. Ensure maintaining the convolution-based constraint structure\n5. Use continuous time-based scheduling for parallelizable execution\n\nOur strategy first derives necessary gradients through theoretical convolution differentiation and then employs:\n\n- A coordinate-wise update framework adapted from convex optimization\n- Automatic handling of boundary conditions [0, 1000]\n- Controlled computational budget management\n- Time-aware fallback mechanisms\n- Full convolution evaluation with exact integrator\n\nThis differs from the parent's approach by focusing computation on gradient-adaptive search and maintaining exact constraint satisfaction. The mathematical basis relies on the AC1 bound definition and treats the problem as continuous optimization with box constraints.\n\nMathematical Specification:\n\nVariables:\n- Sequence: x (n-dimensional)\n- Reparameterization: \u03bc (continuous part), \u03bd (discrete spikes)\n- Normalized sum: s = \u221a(2n \u2211x[i])\n- AC1 score: \u03c6(x) = 2n * |d \u2217 x|/max  / s\u00b2\n\nConstraints:\n1. x[i] \u2208 \u211d \u2200i\n2. 0 \u2264 x[i] \u2264 1000 \u2200i\n3. \u2211x[i] \u2265 0.01 (handled by post-filter)\n\nThe optimization objective is to minimize \u03c6(x).\n\nImplementation Procedure:\n\n1. Initialization:\n- Check parent sequence availability\n- Generate multiple initializations:\n  * Lipschitz-normal initializer\n  * Spectral decomposition + random perturbation\n  * Center-surround pattern generation\n  * Progressive frequency blending\n\n2. Core Iteration:\n- For each initialization:\n  *Diagnostic phase:\n    \u2022 Summarize signal spectrum\n    \u2022 Detect dominant frequencies\n    \u2022 Check extremal value placement\n  *Gradient computation:\n    \u2022 Implicit derivative estimation:\n        d\u03c6/dx[i] \u2248 [\u2207\u03c6](f) \u00b7 [\u2202/\u2202x\u1d62]\n    \u2022 Direction selection based on structure:\n        - Coordinate selection: spatial frequencies\n        - Contour pruning: gradient descent promotion\n  *Projected update:\n    \u2022 x \u2190 \u03bc + reScale(\u03bd)\n    \u2022 \u03bd \u2190 coordinate noise injection\n\n3. Termination Conditions:\n- Monitor score improvements\n- Compute iteration budget: \n  time_limit = \u221a(budget_s)\n- Nominal stability check: \n  (||x_{new} - x_old||/x_old) < \u03b5\n\n4. Structured Initialization Variations:\nWe propose several nested representations:\n1. Tensor-based initialization  \n2. Frequency-adaptive initialization\n3. Multi-scale convolution-initialized patterns\n4. Random feature map initialization\n5. Smooth approximation initializer\n\n5. Budget Management:\n- Compute resource allocation for:\n  1. For most promising candidates: dense computation\n  2. Boundary keepers: sparse maintenance\n  3. Long-term: parallelizable steps\n- Use gradient thresholds to limit inner loop costs\n\n6. Re-evaluation Strategy:\n- Upon completion, evaluate candidate with exact:\n    f = height_sequence(x)\n    score = 2n * max(convolve(f, f)[i] for i) / (sum(f))\u00b2\n- Automatic repair for rejected candidates\n- Fallback parent storage maintenance\n\nThe implementation delegates concrete matrix operations to Numpy, with FFT assisting only for large n. The search is adaptive with real-time termination detection.\n\nDetailed execution steps ensure:\n- All updates respect [0, 1000] bounds\n- Transient candidates eliminate zero-sum cases\n- Adaptable memory management\n- Source consistency with evaluator requirements\n\nWe demonstrate measurable improvement by targeting the bound: \u03c6 \u2264 1.50274349 with the parent's 1.509139 as a reference.\n\nValidation:\n1. Check finite gradient estimates\n2. Confirm normalized sum \u2265 0.01\n3. Compare against exact AC1 evaluation\n4. Track stable improvements\n\nThis is scientific guidance, not a specification. Use your judgment while implementing the core components. Adapt when necessary, but ensure strict adherence to the evaluation contract.",
